// <auto-generated>ANG barks (doc 94): the lines over the head, drawn — never chosen — by the client.</auto-generated>
// The server picked the line, localized it and derived the face from the speaker's caste; this layer only draws it:
//   written (chalk: copper, free folk) letter by letter with a jittered pen and pauses on commas and stops;
//   stamped (stencil: bronze) word by word, each word landing like a stamp; set (matrix: silver) the whole line at once.
// An authored correction is struck out and written above (a restamp for bronze). The line holds for its reading time
// and fades. At most three lines at once, never on top of each other, over the world and under the HUD, a thin thread
// down to the head of the 3D body and a soft shade under the text. The speaker left the frame: the line finishes fast
// and fades in 0.3 s, and a line of somebody out of frame is not started — except a shout to the hall, pinned to the
// screen edge. Glyphs come from the WorldText signed-distance atlases (stencil / chalk / matrix) through ANG/Bark: the
// project has no TextMeshPro, so the mesh-free equivalent of TMP maxVisibleCharacters is a visible-glyph count over a
